concepts:
  - title: "The Four Pillars of Computational Thinking"
    icon: "fa-columns"
    content: |
      <p>Computational thinking is a toolkit of techniques for problem-solving. It's not about thinking like a computer — it's about thinking like a <em>computer scientist</em>.</p>
      
      <div class="pillar-grid">
          <div class="pillar-card">
              <h4>🔍 Abstraction</h4>
              <p>Removing unnecessary detail to focus on what matters. Like the London Tube Map — it ignores real geography to show connections clearly.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pillar-card">
              <h4>🧩 Decomposition</h4>
              <p>Breaking a complex problem into smaller, manageable parts. This supports modularity and allows multiple people to work on different parts.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pillar-card">
              <h4>🔄 Pattern Recognition</h4>
              <p>Identifying similarities in problems. This simplifies solutions by reusing code and developing functions that can be applied multiple times.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pillar-card">
              <h4>📋 Algorithmic Thinking</h4>
              <p>Designing step-by-step instructions that are specific, clear, and in the right order. Every step must be unambiguous.</p>
          </div>
      </div>
      
      <div class="warning-box">
          <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation"></i> Common Mistake</h4>
          <p>Students often define "decomposition" as breaking down a <em>program</em> into smaller sub-programs. This isn't accurate — at the stage decomposition occurs, there is no program yet. You break down the <strong>problem</strong>, not the code.</p>
      </div>

  - title: "Problem Specification"
    icon: "fa-file-lines"
    content: |
      <p>Before writing any code, you must truly understand the problem. A <strong>problem specification</strong> is a formal document that defines:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Stakeholders</strong> — Who is affected by or interested in this problem?</li>
          <li><strong>Problem Statement</strong> — What exactly are we trying to solve?</li>
          <li><strong>Inputs & Outputs</strong> — What data goes in? What results come out?</li>
          <li><strong>Constraints</strong> — What limitations or boundaries exist?</li>
          <li><strong>Success Criteria</strong> — How will we know if our solution works?</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Flowcharts & Trace Tables"
    icon: "fa-diagram-project"
    content: |
      <p>Flowcharts are diagrams used to design and describe algorithms visually. They use standard symbols connected by flowlines to show the execution flow.</p>
      
      <table class="term-table" style="margin: 1rem 0;">
          <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Name</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
          <tr><td>⬭</td><td class="term-name">Terminator</td><td>Start or end of the process</td></tr>
          <tr><td>▱</td><td class="term-name">Input/Output</td><td>Input or output of data</td></tr>
          <tr><td>▭</td><td class="term-name">Process</td><td>Action, calculation, or assignment</td></tr>
          <tr><td>◇</td><td class="term-name">Decision</td><td>Yes/No or True/False branching</td></tr>
          <tr><td>→</td><td class="term-name">Flowline</td><td>Direction of data flow</td></tr>
      </table>
      
      <p>A <strong>trace table</strong> (or "dry run") tracks how variables change as you step through an algorithm. It's used to verify your algorithm produces the expected output.</p>
      
      <div class="warning-box">
          <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation"></i> Don't Forget</h4>
          <p>Always label decision branches with <strong>Yes/No</strong> or <strong>True/False</strong>. Never leave flowlines disconnected — every path must lead somewhere.</p>
      </div>

